#!/usr/bin/env bash
# SM/BRST section guards — an aggressor gate over the *prose and declaration*
# pairs whose "simplification" is to delete one side.
#
# Background.  Four of the recent SM sections state a result together with the
# boundary that stops it from being read as more than it is, and in each case the
# cheap way to make a later edit look green is to drop one half of the pair:
#
#   1. §44/§45 (parity reduction) — `deficiencyTrivialAt_iff_sectors` reduces the
#      closure problem to the two parity sectors, but P4 does **not** decompose
#      over the split: `sectorComparison_iff_formDomainComparison` says the §32
#      named input read on a sector is the whole statement, so the input is one
#      problem rather than two (and, since §53, a *proved* one).  Resolving the
#      tension by dropping either the sector reduction or the "no weakening"
#      verdict is the failure mode.
#   2. §46/§47 (BRST) — the charge form is well defined on the cohomology
#      (`kform_well_defined`), and `PhysicalPositivity` is *derived* from quartet
#      data (`physicalPositivity_of_quartet`).  Since §55 the Standard Model's own
#      quartet data exists (`smQuartet`, `smBghost`), but its projector's range is
#      not identified with the physical subspace.  Dropping either the derivation
#      or that surviving caveat is the failure mode.
#   3. The QYM field-strength split — the Pauli-coordinate entry checks run only
#      under `set_option maxHeartbeats 1000000 in`, and the dense coloured core
#      (`dense_colouredCore`) is what makes the split a statement about the core.
#      Deleting the entry checks, or the denseness, "resolves" the conflict.
#   4. CONSOLIDATED_PLAN.md — the QYM Stone-flow row and the meson/glueball
#      square entries are the two-way record of that track; a plan edit that
#      keeps one and drops the other is the conflict silently closed.
#   5. §48/§49 (gradient domination) — `GradDom` holds for a coercive quartic
#      (`gradDom_coercive_quartic`) and **fails** for the flat `X₀²X₁²`
#      (`not_gradDom_flat_quartic`), which is why the structural route of §49
#      (`pointwise_dom_of_sq_sum`) carries the coercive quadratic term
#      (`gradDom_flat_plus_quadratic`).  Keeping the coercion theorem and
#      dropping the flat counterexample — or vice versa — turns "the flat
#      pattern is real and needs an argument" into "the pattern does not occur",
#      which is the failure mode: the counterexample is what makes the §49
#      hypothesis non-vacuous.
#   6. §54 (downstream + QYM/NS) — `exists_potSelfBound_sm` is *used* (hpoly and the P2 bound are
#      instantiated), and the same normal form is shown **not** to give gradient domination for the
#      QYM potential (`not_gradDom_weylFlat`).  Keeping the instantiation and dropping the
#      obstruction — or vice versa — reads the generic machinery as a generic theorem, which is the
#      failure mode.
#   7. §55/§56 (the SM's own b-ghost) — `smBghost`/`smQuartet` exist, §56 characterises
#      the projector's range (`range V = ker Q ⊓ ker P`) and derives positivity from
#      positivity *on* that range, *and* the construction is choice-based: no mode
#      pairing, hence no identification with the transverse parity-even subspace.
#      Deleting the construction oversells the gap; deleting the caveat oversells the
#      construction (a characterised range is not a no-ghost theorem).
#   8. §57 (the b-ghost formula) — `canonicalHomotopy` is an explicit, choice-free b-ghost and
#      its projector's range is the harmonic subspace `ker Q ⊓ (range Q)ᗮ`, *and* ghost parity is
#      still missing and positivity on that range is still the input.  Deleting the formula
#      oversells the gap; deleting the caveats turns a transverse range theorem into a no-ghost
#      theorem.
#   9. §55 (the converse + the quartet boundary) — `gradDom_weylCoercive_flat` is the
#      *converse* of `not_gradDom_weylFlat`: the same QYM potential with the coercive
#      quadratic added does obey the bound.  Dropping it leaves the §54 obstruction
#      reading as a limitation of the method rather than of the model.  Separately, the
#      §55 text must keep saying that the SM quartet data is still unbuilt, since the
#      attempt to build it was withdrawn and no claim was upgraded.
#   10. §55 pairing (Kleene-style) — §54's `not_gradDom_weylFlat` and §55's
#      `gradDom_weylCoercive_flat` are one statement in two halves: *this* model
#      fails the domination and *this* model with the quadratic term satisfies it.
#      A plain "both needles exist" test is not enough, because the two needles
#      can survive in different sections while the prose that ties them together
#      is rewritten: the pair can be split rather than deleted.  So the check is
#      a co-occurrence window (`pair_require`): both sides must sit within
#      §55's width of each other, `not_gradDom_weylFlat` first.  Failing to find
#      either side, or finding them far apart, fails — the two ways of silently
#      turning a sharp obstruction into a general one.
#   11. §56 (NS: reduction + sextic) — the NS Lagrangian potential's domination is
#      *reduced* to the volume penalty's own estimate (`lagGradDom_of_volDom`, with
#      `VolDom` named), and the penalty's input is proved not to be free
#      (`not_gradDom_flatSextic`, the non-negative degree-six polynomial that no
#      `a, b` dominate).  Dropping the sextic turns `VolDom` into bookkeeping;
#      dropping the reduction loses the model's free coercive blocks.  Same
#      co-occurrence discipline as item 10, over §56.
#
# The gate is deliberately textual: it cannot certify mathematics, only that a
# later change did not *remove* the half of a pair that carries the caveat.
#
# Exit 0 = all guarded pairs intact, 1 = at least one side missing.

set -u
repo_root="$(cd "$(dirname "$0")/.." && pwd)"
cd "$repo_root" || exit 1

FAILED=0
fail() {
    echo "[FAIL] $1"
    FAILED=1
}

# `require <file> <needle> <why>` — the file must exist and contain the literal.
require() {
    local file="$1" needle="$2" why="$3"
    if [ ! -f "$file" ]; then
        fail "$file missing ($why)"
        return
    fi
    if ! grep -qF -- "$needle" "$file"; then
        fail "$file no longer contains '$needle' ($why)"
    fi
}

# `pair_require <file> <first> <second> <window> <why>` — both literals must occur in the
# file, `first` before `second`, within `window` lines of each other.  This is the
# Kleene-style half of the gate: "not both absent" is checked by `require`, and the
# adjacency here checks "not separated", so the pair cannot be resolved by splitting it.
pair_require() {
    local file="$1" first="$2" second="$3" window="$4" why="$5"
    if [ ! -f "$file" ]; then
        fail "$file missing ($why)"
        return
    fi
    local a b
    a=$(grep -nF -- "$first" "$file" | head -1 | cut -d: -f1)
    b=$(grep -nF -- "$second" "$file" | head -1 | cut -d: -f1)
    if [ -z "$a" ]; then
        fail "$file no longer contains '$first' ($why)"
        return
    fi
    if [ -z "$b" ]; then
        fail "$file no longer contains '$second' ($why)"
        return
    fi
    if [ "$a" -ge "$b" ]; then
        fail "$file states '$second' before '$first' ($why)"
        return
    fi
    if [ $((b - a)) -gt "$window" ]; then
        fail "$file separates '$first' (line $a) from '$second' (line $b) by more than \
$window lines ($why)"
        return
    fi
}

